    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">333 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> loses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Battle of Issus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a>, who captures his wife, mother and two daughters</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">332 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> after a siege from January to July and then Gaza, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> surrenders to him</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">331 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> makes a third offer of terms, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> refuses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">331 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> marches from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">331 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The armies meet at Gaugamela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> leads a wedge into the Persian centre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">331 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> flees and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> is surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-gaugamela" data-v="76f037d638" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies stand drawn up</b> The Persians were already on the field. Darius stood in the centre, Bessus commanded the left wing and Mazaeus the right, with chariots in front. Alexander held the right of the Macedonians and Parmenion the left, with the phalanx between them in a double line.</li><li><b>The phalanx advances and Alexander rides to the right</b> Alexander ordered the infantry to march towards the centre of the enemy line, with the wings echeloned back. He himself rode towards the edge of the right flank with the Companions, to draw the Persian cavalry outwards.</li><li><b>The Persian wings attack</b> The Scythian cavalry of the Persian left tried to get round Alexander's extreme right, and a long cavalry fight followed there. Darius also sent a large part of his cavalry against Parmenion on the left.</li><li><b>The chariots charge</b> Darius launched his chariots at the troops under Alexander. Javelin-throwers stopped many, and the Macedonian ranks opened to let the rest pass harmlessly. Those that got through were then killed.</li><li><b>Alexander's wedge strikes the Persian centre</b> Alexander drew his Companions out of the fight, formed a large wedge with other units and led it into the Persian centre, while the phalanx kept the Persian infantry engaged. By the widely held view, Darius then broke and fled.</li><li><b>Parmenion is hard pressed and a gap opens</b> Parmenion's wing was apparently encircled and sent desperate messages, and Alexander turned back to help instead of pursuing Darius. A gap had opened in the phalanx, and Persian and Indian cavalry broke through towards the camp.</li><li><b>The raiders are dispersed and the Persian right gives way</b> The rear reserve phalanx attacked and dispersed the raiders as they looted. Alexander fought the Persian right-wing cavalry and Mazaeus began to pull his forces back, and they fell into disorder under the Thessalian charge.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-iii.jpg" alt="Darius III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_III_mosaic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In November 333 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Battle of Issus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a>, who captured Darius's wife, his mother Sisygambis and his two daughters and gained southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. Alexander then besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> from January to July 332 BCE and fought at Gaza. Many Persian troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had been withdrawn for Issus and had died there, so the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazaces">Mazaces</a> surrendered Egypt peacefully.</p><p>Ancient historians give conflicting accounts of Darius's attempts to negotiate, which a modern scholar, Nawotka, calls hypothetical to reconstruct. Three attempts are usually distinguished. Darius reportedly offered to ransom his family, first for 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, then to cede land west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a> river, and then to accept Alexander as co-equal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> alone says Alexander hid one letter and showed his friends a forged one. Alexander is said to have refused all offers.</p><p>The third offer, made after Alexander left Egypt, was larger: land west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, co-rulership, a daughter's hand and 30,000 talents. Diodorus says Parmenion advised acceptance, and Alexander answered, "So should I, if I were Parmenion." Alexander insisted there could be only one king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and called on Darius to surrender or to fight. The ancient authors differ on details: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> give the ransom as 10,000 talents, the others 30,000, and Arrian places the third attempt during the siege of Tyre. Darius then prepared for another battle.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Gaugamela&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Negotiations between Darius and Alexander, Prelude, Alexander's march through Mesopotamia:</p><ul><li>Peter Green, Alexander of Macedon, 356–323 B.C.: A Historical Biography (University of California Press, 2013)</li><li>Kaveh Farrokh, Shadows in the desert: ancient Persia at war (Osprey Publishing, 2007)</li><li>Diodorus Siculus, Library of History (Harvard University Press, 1963)</li><li>Bill Yenne, Alexander the Great: Lessons From History's Undefeated General (Palgrave MacMillan, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry.jpg" alt="Companion cavalry" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Issus_mosaic_-_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_-_Naples_2013-05-16_16-25-06_BW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> commanded Greek forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and the Hellenic League, with Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and levies from the tributary Paeonians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> gives 7,000 cavalry and 40,000 infantry, and there is general acceptance of his numbers among modern scholars. Most historians agree that the army had 31,000 heavy infantry, including mercenaries and the allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> kept in reserve, and 9,000 light infantry, mainly peltasts with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>.</p><p>For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a>'s army, modern scholars reject the ancient figures. Arrian gives 40,000 cavalry and 1,000,000 infantry, Diodorus 200,000 cavalry and 800,000 infantry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> 1,000,000 without a breakdown, and Curtius Rufus 45,000 cavalry and 200,000 infantry; Arrian, Diodorus and Curtius give 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and Arrian 15 elephants. Worthington calls these numbers absurdly high, and Bichler says the only common ground among modern authors is that they are of no use. Warry estimates 91,000, Harbottle 120,000, and Engels and Green no more than 100,000.</p><p>Most of Darius's troops were of lower quality than Alexander's. His better infantry were about 2,000 Greek mercenary hoplites and the 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immortals">Immortals</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were armed in the Greek way. He had recruited the best cavalry of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, and had the ground levelled and cleared of bushes for his scythed chariots. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessus">Bessus</a> commanded the left flank and Mazaeus the right, with Darius in the centre, as Persian kings traditionally did. Alexander took the right with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a> and Parmenion the left.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hypaspists"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hypaspists.jpg" alt="Hypaspists" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hypaspists">Hypaspists</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonian_Army_Pezetairos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> advanced with his wings drawn back at 45 degrees to lure the Persian cavalry. While the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanxes</a> engaged the Persian infantry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> sent much of his cavalry and some infantry against Parmenion on the left, and Alexander rode to the edge of the right with his Companions, trying to draw the Persian cavalry outwards and open a gap in the centre. Darius's Scythian and Bactrian cavalry attacked Alexander's right in a long fight in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a>, greatly outnumbered, were often hard pressed; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> says more of Alexander's men fell there.</p><p>Darius then launched his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>; javelin throwers stopped many, and ranks opened to let the rest pass before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypaspists">hypaspists</a> and grooms killed them. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> pressed on to the flanks, Alexander formed a wedge of Companions, the guard brigade and phalanx battalions he could spare, and struck the weakened Persian centre, taking out the royal guard and the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The widely held modern view, based on Arrian, is that Darius then fled and the army followed.</p><p>Alexander did not pursue at once. Desperate messages came from Parmenion, whose wing seemed encircled by the Persian right. Alexander turned back, and Arrian describes the fiercest fighting of the day as he met Indian and Parthian cavalry and the bravest Persian division trying to break through; sixty Companions were killed. Meanwhile a gap had opened in the Macedonian line, and Persian and Indian cavalry broke through to loot the camp, where the rear phalanx dispersed them; Sisygambis refused to leave with them. Mazaeus then pulled back.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/persepolis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persepolis.jpg" alt="Persepolis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018-09-21_Iran,_Persepolis,_Tachara_(from_the_southeast).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Laurens R. Krol, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bagophanes formally surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a>. Parmenion captured the Persian baggage train, and Alexander and his bodyguard pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a>. The booty included 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, the king's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> and bow, and the elephants. Sixty of the Companions were killed.</p><p>Darius escaped on horseback with a small body of men. The Bactrian cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessus">Bessus</a> caught up with him, as did survivors of the Royal Guard and 2,000 Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. At that point the Persian Empire was divided into an eastern and a western half. Darius planned to go further east and raise another army, and wrote to the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> to ask them to stay loyal.</p><p>The satraps had other intentions. Bessus murdered Darius and fled east. Alexander is said to have been saddened to see an enemy he respected killed so, gave Darius a full burial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, and pursued Bessus, whom he captured and executed the following year. Most of the remaining satraps gave their loyalty to Alexander and kept their positions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> is traditionally considered to have ended with Darius's death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurel-stein.jpg" alt="Aurel Stein" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein">Aurel Stein</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurel_Stein_1909.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thompson, The Grosvenor Studios, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ancient accounts are by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, Curtius Rufus, Justin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, writing centuries after the battle; Justin, Arrian and Curtius wrote in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE. They are sometimes inconsistent and give no precise topography, so the location of the field is not known with certainty. The sites proposed include Tel Gomel, Karamlesh, Qaraqosh, Tell Aswad and a mound south of Wardak. The most commonly accepted is Karamlesh, suggested by Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein">Aurel Stein</a> in 1938; all lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> Plains east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a>.</p><p>The date is a calculation. After the army crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> a near total lunar eclipse took place on 20 to 21 September 331 BCE; four days later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a>'s men captured scouts of Mazaeus, and allowing four days' rest, the battle must have been on 1 October. One modern reconstruction relies heavily on the Babylonian astronomical diaries.</p><p>Modern historians disagree on the size of the Persian army and on why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> chose the ground. Peter Green thinks Alexander's northern route caught the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> off guard, and that Darius, using scorched earth and scythed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, wanted to repeat Cunaxa; Jona Lendering argues that Darius led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> to Gaugamela on purpose, as the Persians' preferred battlefield. The episode of Parmenion's desperate messages was later used by Callisthenes and others to discredit Parmenion.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Gaugamela&#8221; cites where it covers Strategic analysis, Location, Ancient sources:</p><ul><li>Stephen English, The Field Campaigns of Alexander the Great (Pen &amp; Sword, 2010)</li><li>Peter Green, Alexander of Macedon, 356–323 B.C.: A Historical Biography (University of California Press, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gaugamela was the second and last battle between the two kings. It is considered to be the final blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> then conquered completely, and it is described as a decisive victory for the League of Corinth that led to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius</a> and of the empire. The empire was left divided into eastern and western halves when Darius fled, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> who killed him were mostly allowed to keep their posts under Alexander.</p><p>The victory is credited to superior tactics and the clever use of light infantry against a much larger army. Alexander's plan of drawing the Persian cavalry to the flanks and then striking at the gap in the centre is described as an unusual strategy that has been duplicated only a few times, needing almost perfect timing. The Persian use of levelled ground and scythed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, by contrast, is read by Peter Green as an attempt to repeat the earlier battle of Cunaxa.</p><p>The battle has continued to be studied. Marciak and others have published work on Alexander's route to Gaugamela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Arbela</a>, on the visibility of the battlefield, and on the battle in the Navkur Plain in the context of Macedonian and Persian warfare, and the question of the site remains open.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Gaugamela in 331 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, whose army ancient writers put at up to a million men and modern historians at roughly 91,000 to 120,000. Darius fled and was later murdered, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> came to an end.</em></p>

